<Layout><section class="hero wrap"><p class="eyebrow">A PRACTICAL GUIDE TO PHOTO FORMATS</p><h1>RAW vs JPEG.<br/><span>See the trade-off.</span></h1><div class="hero-bottom"><p>More editing freedom. Smaller files. Different trade-offs.<br/>Compare real camera samples and estimate your photo storage.</p><a class="primary-button" href="#compare">Explore the difference <span>↓</span></a></div></section><Comparison/><section class="wrap decision"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE QUICK DECISION</p><h2>Choose for the way you shoot.</h2></div><article><span class="format-tag">RAW</span><h3>Room to work.</h3><p>Keep sensor data for flexible editing. Useful when lighting is tricky, the shot matters, or you want control over the final look.</p></article><article><span class="format-tag">JPEG</span><h3>Ready to go.</h3><p>Smaller files with a finished look. Useful for fast sharing, simple workflows, and photographs you don’t plan to edit extensively.</p></article><article><span class="format-tag">RAW + JPEG</span><h3>A little of both.</h3><p>A ready-to-share image and an editable original. Convenient, with extra storage and more files to manage.</p></article></section><Calculator/><section class="wrap guides"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / CAMERA FIELD NOTES</p><h2>Start with your camera.</h2><p>One focused guide to begin. More only when the data is ready.</p></div><a class="guide-card" href="/canon-r6-mark-ii-raw-vs-jpeg/"><span class="eyebrow">CANON · FULL-FRAME</span><h3>EOS R6 Mark II</h3><p>RAW vs JPEG · measured file size · storage planning</p><span class="guide-arrow">Read the field notes ↗</span></a></section></Layout>
